//! `IoFuture` and the executor operations of an `Io`.
//!
//! An `IoFuture` is what every `std/sys` operation hands back, and `io.await`
//! turns it into an `i32`. Every operation takes the `Io` it runs on as its
//! last argument and calls that `Io`'s table (`io.vtable.*.<op>(io.ctx, …)`,
//! `IoVTable` in the prelude), so the `Io` a caller passes decides which
//! event loop and reactor the operation goes to
//! (`plans/backlog/IO_INTERFACE_AND_STD_RUNTIME.md` §2). This module holds the
//! executor's operations (`park`, `yield`) and `completed`; the file, socket,
//! process, DNS, timer and watch operations live beside it in `std/sys`.
//! The public modules above (`std/fs`, `std/net`, `std/process`,
//! `std/time/sleep`, `std/async`) forward the `io` they already hold.
//!
//! ## Stability
//!
//! unstable — this is an ABI, not an API. `__yo_io_future_t` is emitted by
//! the compiler's own runtime (`src/codegen/async/`) with a layout the await
//! codegen indexes directly (`state`, `result`), so its shape is pinned to
//! the async state-machine representation and moves with it, not with a
//! library decision (the plan's §3 is the contract). Its *value* convention
//! is one thing on every platform: a resolved `i32` that is a non-negative
//! syscall result or a negative errno, with `sys/dns` alone resolving to its
//! documented `DNS_ERR_*` codes (above every errno). The raw `i32` stays — a
//! typed `Result(i32, IoError)` channel at this boundary was considered and
//! declined (`plans/archive/ASYNC_IO_API_AUDIT.md` Q2): `IoError.check` and
//! `IoError.from_result` turn the answer into a value at the call site. The
//! operations' own shape is not final either: the allocation-free async plan
//! starts them in a slot the caller owns
//! (`plans/backlog/ALLOCATION_FREE_ASYNC.md` AFA-8). An `IoFuture` is
//! therefore awaited where it is made (`io.await(IO_file.read(…, io), io)`),
//! never bound and awaited later.
pragma(Pragma.AllowUnsafe);
extern(
  "Yo",
  __yo_io_future_completed : (fn(result : i32) -> IoFuture)
);
/// A future that suspends the task until a `Waker` made from it fires
/// (`std/async/waker`'s `Park`). No backend ever completes it on its own.
park :: (fn(io : Io) -> IoFuture)(
  io.vtable.*.park(io.ctx)
);
/// A future that is pending now and completed by the loop's next ready-task
/// drain: one turn of the event loop, with one I/O poll in between. It is
/// never an already-completed future, because an await takes the inline fast
/// path for one of those and the task would never leave the C stack.
yield :: (fn(io : Io) -> IoFuture)(
  io.vtable.*.yield(io.ctx)
);
/// A future born completed with `result`: what an `Io` whose answer is known
/// at once returns from an operation. An await of it takes the inline fast
/// path, so nothing ever schedules it.
completed :: (fn(result : i32) -> IoFuture)(
  __yo_io_future_completed(result)
);
export(IoFuture, park, yield, completed);
